# Enforce the engines range instead of merely declaring it. Without this npm
# treats `engines` as advisory, so a wrong Node major builds and tests happily
# and only shows up as odd behaviour later. The range is >=24 <25 in all three
# manifests that declare it (root, apps/web, packages/sdk), matching the
# production container (ops/docker/Dockerfile pins node:24-bookworm-slim) and
# CI.
#
# Consequence worth knowing: a shell on a different major — Homebrew's node
# 25.x, say — now fails npm outright rather than silently building against a
# major we do not ship. That is the point, but it reads as a regression to
# anyone who hits it, so `nvm use` (the repo's .nvmrc says 24) is the fix.
#
# Checked before enabling: no dependency in node_modules declares an engines
# range that rejects Node 24 (96 distinct values scanned to depth 4), so
# engine-strict cannot fail an install on a transitive constraint.
engine-strict=true
